## What the law says (version in force on 1 October 2025)

The version in force on 1 October 2025 is the *Residential Tenancies Act 1997*, file "rta-1997-v110-2025-08-06.pdf" (6 August 2025). The file name indicates Authorised Version No. 110, but the excerpt I retrieved doesn't show the cover page. The next version in the documents is v111, dated 25 November 2025 [[5]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-11/97-109aa111-authorised.pdf), so v110 applies to your date.

- **Fixed rent.** An agent or provider must not advertise or offer a property unless the rent is advertised or offered as a fixed amount [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf).
- **No inviting higher offers.** They must not "solicit or otherwise invite an offer of an amount of rent that is higher than the advertised amount." The penalty is 60 penalty units for a natural person and 300 for a body corporate [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf).
- **Accepting unsolicited higher offers.** In v110 this is not prohibited. The ban on accepting an unsolicited or uninvited higher offer (s 30F(4)) appears only in v111 (25 November 2025), where it is noted as inserted by No. 6/2025 [[5]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-11/97-109aa111-authorised.pdf). That is after your date, so I can't say it applied to you. A later Regulations document (dated 30 June 2026) also describes this ban [[2]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2026-06/21-3sra009-authorised.pdf), but it is also after your date.
- **Misleading advertising.** An agent or provider must not make a false or misleading representation about the rent when promoting or advertising premises (s 30G) [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf).
- **Misleading conduct.** They must not induce a person to enter an agreement by misleading or deceptive conduct, including false representations about the rent (s 30E) [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf).
- **Discrimination.** A provider must not refuse to let premises on the basis of an attribute under the Equal Opportunity Act 2010. A person who suffers loss from discrimination may apply to the Tribunal for compensation [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf).

## What you can do or ask

- **Challenge the facts.** You can ask the agent in writing why your application was recorded as withdrawn, and ask them to correct it.
- **Rely on the rules above if they fit.** Your case may involve:
  - an agent inviting higher offers (s 30F(3));
  - a misleading rent advertisement (s 30G);
  - discrimination, if the refusal was based on a protected attribute.
- **Limits of the documents.** Nothing I retrieved covers:
  - a provision about falsely recording an application as "withdrawn";
  - whether you can force the agent to give you the tenancy;
  - how to enforce s 30F or s 30G. The text I found gives only penalties.

  I can't confirm those remedies from the documents.

## What to save

The documents don't list evidence. As general practical suggestions, keep:
- the original advertisement (screenshots showing the advertised rent and date);
- the later listing at the higher rent;
- your application and its submission confirmation;
- the "withdrawn" email, with full headers and date;
- all messages with the agent;
- anything showing who said a higher offer was made, and whether the agent invited it.
